insert into public.posts (id, campaign_id, theme, position, body, linkedin_url, status, scheduled_at, posted_at)
values ('5449a1ab-23ba-4c81-8056-41dacb99e312', 'f34a2607-06a3-4c4b-8e3d-2315bc92afb5', 'The Question I Ask Before Starting Any New Feature', 9, 'The Question I Ask Before Starting Any New Feature

Whenever we discuss a new feature at CipherCross, I start with a single question:

 “Will this make someone’s life calmer, easier, or healthier?”

If the answer is no, we don’t build it.

It sounds simple, but it wasn’t always how I approached product decisions. My journey from valuing complexity to prioritizing user well-being has been transformative.

Early in my career, I thought great products were measured by how many features they had, the more buttons, dashboards, and integrations - the better.

But over time, I realized something important: complexity doesn’t equal value. In fact, it often does the opposite.
It overwhelms, distracts, and burns people out. Our focus on simplicity reassures our users that we''re not adding unnecessary complexity to their lives.

I’ve seen brilliant teams spend months perfecting a feature, only to discover that no one needed it.
I’ve also seen how a single slight improvement - one that removes friction or stress - can completely change the way someone experiences a product.

That’s why I keep coming back to this guiding question.
It''s a beacon that guides us, forcing us to step out of the “builder mindset” and think like the person who will use what we create.

Because at the end of the day, our work isn’t about adding more technology into the world. It’s about building technology that quietly supports people’s lives.

 — That helps them focus.
 — That gives them peace of mind.
 — That reduces stress instead of adding to it.

When we frame our roadmap around this principle, decision-making gets clearer. We don’t chase trends just because competitors are doing them. We don’t prioritize “flashy” features that look good in a demo but deliver little value.

Instead, we prioritize the human outcome.

This doesn’t always mean saying no is easy. Sometimes it means killing ideas we loved. Sometimes it means slowing down when everything around us says, “move faster.”
But every time we stick to this principle, the result is a product that people genuinely want to use - because it makes their lives better, not more complicated.

 My advice to anyone building: before you invest your time, your team’s energy, and your users’ attention - pause and ask:
“Will this make life calmer, easier, or healthier?”

If the answer is no, it’s probably not worth building.', null, 'posted', '2025-10-22T12:00:00Z', '2025-10-22T12:00:00Z');
