values ('42b95271-6f28-4e5a-96ba-ea50b0f908e2', 'f34a2607-06a3-4c4b-8e3d-2315bc92afb5', 'Building wellness apps that truly support people', 21, 'When you spend years building wellness apps,
you can’t ignore your own health.

At CipherCross, we’ve helped clients design products that track sleep, encourage better nutrition, and guide people toward calmer routines.

But somewhere along the way, I realized:
I wasn’t applying these principles to myself.

Like many founders, I used to push through long nights, skip breaks, and treat wellness as something I’d “get to later.”
The irony wasn’t lost on me - I was helping create tools for healthier living while ignoring my own.

So I started practicing what we preach.

 I began tracking my sleep, and saw the direct link between rest and decision-making.
 I started taking mindful breaks during the day - not scrolling, but truly stepping away.
 I paid more attention to small rituals that bring balance - hydration, breathing, and reflection.

None of this made me “perfectly healthy.” But it made me more present, sharper in my work, and calmer in moments of pressure.

And here’s the unexpected lesson: building wellness apps didn’t just change how I see users’ needs - it changed how I see my own.

Sometimes technology is not just about serving others, but about holding a mirror to ourselves.

 Has your work ever influenced your personal habits in surprising ways?

(Photo: one of my mindful breaks: yes, lying down on the mat counts too.)', 'https://www.linkedin.com/posts/mykyta-shevchenko-ciphercross-wellness_wellnesstech-userexperience-empathyindesign-activity-7445009680617312256-dpWm?utm_source=share&utm_medium=member_desktop&rcm=ACoAACKi8XsB9WBoWK1z6g7nRgrBaW3-4QoyJT8', 'posted', '2026-04-01T07:30:00Z', '2026-04-01T07:30:00Z');
