values ('49305b74-9926-4843-a271-80d622cb1543', '969b6828-7641-4509-9a99-50ec4877e157', '4 Full-Time Jobs at Once? Here’s What It Really Teaches You', 1, 'Format: Micro-case / Short story, Essay Type: Knowledge Hook: At one point, I was working 4 full-time jobs simultaneously. It wasn’t about hustle culture. It was survival — and transformation.', null, 'posted', '2025-08-15T08:00:00Z', '2025-08-15T08:00:00Z');
insert into public.posts (id, campaign_id, theme, position, body, linkedin_url, status, scheduled_at, posted_at)
values ('b8b387ad-c6f5-4f2d-8ab4-f97248352ef8', '969b6828-7641-4509-9a99-50ec4877e157', 'Dancing, Diving & Drifting: The Unexpected Sides of a Tech Co-Founder', 2, 'Dancing, Diving & Drifting: Finding Creative Fuel Beyond the Project

 Not every tech founder’s story
 should start with their job description.

 Here’s a look at the sides of me
 you won’t see on an app’s roadmap,
 and why they keep my mind and
 energy fresh for building CipherCross.

 Routines that make me relaxed:

1. Early mornings: Descending to old shipwrecks,
 I disappear from inboxes and responsibilities.
 Underwater, it’s all about slow breathing and silent focus.

2. Night moves: Berlin’s techno scene is where
 I lose any built-up stress from the week.
 No roles, no hierarchy—just rhythm, friends, and movement.
 Here, I’m not a founder, just another person in the crowd,
 letting off steam and remembering what it means to be truly present.

3. Weekend drift: Fast cars and control
 of a perfect drift demands pure attention.
 Every split second counts, forcing me to
 confront risk, adapt instantly, and, sometimes,
 just laugh at my own mistakes. It’s electrifying.

 Why is the presence of these activities important to me?

 Stepping outside work, I find lessons I can bring back:

→ Adaptability to facing new underwater challenges.
 → Letting go and regaining energy with music and dance.
 → Focus and comfort with risk behind the wheel.

 Each adventure is its form of reset.
 By Monday, I will return to my team with
 creativity fueled, ego checked,
 and motivation renewed—not because
 I worked harder, but because I lived more fully.

 What’s something outside your usual path that
 gives you a new perspective each week?
 If you’ve got a story, lesson, or ritual that
 shapes who you are at work - let’s talk.', null, 'posted', '2025-08-18T08:00:00Z', '2025-08-18T08:00:00Z');
insert into public.posts (id, campaign_id, theme, position, body, linkedin_url, status, scheduled_at, posted_at)
values ('09e9c392-1097-4e55-9a31-3b2c6199e765', '969b6828-7641-4509-9a99-50ec4877e157', 'What Working Remotely Around the World Taught Me About Leadership', 3, 'What Working Remotely Around the World Taught Me About Leadership

There’s something magical about starting your day in one timezone
 while your teammates log on from three others.

I used to think remote work was just about WiFi, Slack, and juggling calendars.

But after years of working (and sometimes wandering) across continents,
I realized the real lesson isn’t about digital tools.
It’s about trust.

Trust that your teammate in Berlin will meet the deadline -
even if you’re asleep in Bali.

Trust that a late-night idea dropped into the group chat
won’t get lost in the noise.

I’ve learned leadership isn’t about hovering or micromanaging.

You plant the vision and let people grow in their own way.

You don’t watch the clock -you watch what people build when they feel free.

Sometimes, that means embracing the unpredictability:
Missed calls, power cuts, and creative solutions born at odd hours.

But the best work, and the best teams, aren’t defined by office walls.

They’re built on clarity, respect, and space to do things differently.

And in those moments between calls - on a train, in a new city, or just out for a walk -
I’ve found that unexpected sparks often turn into the most exciting projects.

Leadership, in a remote world, is really about letting go -
So everyone has room to show who they are.

Because the greatest distance you’ll ever bridge isn’t time zones.
It’s the space between doing things the old way and discovering what teams can do
When you trust them fully.

RemoteLeadership #Trust #WorkFromAnywhere #TechLife #TeamCulture #CipherCross #PersonalGrowth', null, 'posted', '2025-08-19T08:00:00Z', '2025-08-19T08:00:00Z');
insert into public.posts (id, campaign_id, theme, position, body, linkedin_url, status, scheduled_at, posted_at)
values ('77e0ab23-da91-49fc-8a02-e3803eb4ce34', '969b6828-7641-4509-9a99-50ec4877e157', 'Advice to My Younger Self (That Still Guides Me Today)', 4, 'Format: List / Reflection Type: Personal Hook: If I could talk to 18-year-old me, I’d say 5 words: “Buy bitcoin. Trust your gut.”', null, 'posted', '2025-08-20T08:00:00Z', '2025-08-20T08:00:00Z');
insert into public.posts (id, campaign_id, theme, position, body, linkedin_url, status, scheduled_at, posted_at)
values ('2ca28594-3568-40c4-aafc-e09265804134', '969b6828-7641-4509-9a99-50ec4877e157', 'Taking Responsibility, Owning Your Path', 5, 'Waiting for someone to tell you what to do?
That’s your first mistake.

Early in my journey, I sometimes found myself hanging back.
Checking for the green light, waiting for a playbook, hoping for someone to point out the next step.

Spoiler:
Nobody arrived with the answer.

The biggest shifts in my career (and life) came when I started owning my path.

Not by knowing it all.
But by making the first move - even if it felt awkward, uncertain, or a little messy.

Responsibility isn’t a box you tick.
It’s the decision to show up, ask harder questions, run the risky experiments, and stand behind the outcome - win or lose.

Nobody else cares about your potential as much as you.
The mentors, the leaders, the friends - they help.
But it’s your choice to make things happen.

I’ve seen this across every great team:
The people who move things forward aren’t the loudest or the most qualified.
They’re the ones who quietly take responsibility.
Own the gaps.
Turn “someone should” into “I will.”

You don’t need permission to care more, try something new, or outgrow your comfort zone.

If you’re waiting for a sign,
This is it.

Take responsibility.
Own your path.
You’ll be surprised how far it carries you.

Leadership #PersonalGrowth #Responsibility #Ownership #Entrepreneurship #CipherCross #KeepMoving', null, 'posted', '2025-08-25T08:00:00Z', '2025-08-25T08:00:00Z');
insert into public.posts (id, campaign_id, theme, position, body, linkedin_url, status, scheduled_at, posted_at)
values ('ed5c9883-4f16-446e-82c1-76f63d144658', '969b6828-7641-4509-9a99-50ec4877e157', 'Innovation Means Nothing Without Humans', 6, 'Format: Story + framework (3 steps to keep innovation human-first) Type: Business / Knowledge Hook: "You can build the most advanced tech in the world — but if it’s not serving people, it’s just code."', null, 'posted', '2025-09-15T12:00:00Z', '2025-09-15T12:00:00Z');
insert into public.posts (id, campaign_id, theme, position, body, linkedin_url, status, scheduled_at, posted_at)
values ('24fe6c90-4a88-4810-8279-b22b486200fe', '969b6828-7641-4509-9a99-50ec4877e157', 'Why I Built My Company to Benefit from Uncertainty', 7, 'When I first read The Black Swan by Nassim Nicholas Taleb, I wasn’t just reading a book.
 I was having my thinking flipped upside down.

Most businesses I knew were built to survive uncertainty.
 To keep operating when the market gets unpredictable.
 To “make it through” until things get stable again.

But here’s the thing: the world doesn’t get stable.
 There’s no “normal” we’re returning to.
 Uncertainty isn’t an exception - it’s the default.

 Taleb introduced me to the concept of antifragility.
 It’s a mindset where you don’t just endure shocks - you actually grow stronger because
 of them.
 Like a muscle that gets bigger after stress.

 That single idea changed how I approached business forever.

 When I co-founded CipherCross, I built it with one question in mind:
 How do we not just survive change… but make it our advantage?

 For us, that meant:

→ Structuring the company to be lean but highly skilled.
→ Building processes that can pivot quickly.
→ Staying curious and ready to experiment - even in high-pressure situations.

 This isn’t theory.

 When the AI wave hit, we didn’t wait for the trend to “settle down.”

 Within hours, we tested, built, and launched
 a working prototype - not because we were under pressure,
 but because we thrive on speed, adaptability, and bold experimentation.

 It’s in our DNA.

 The result?
  We’re not scared when things shift.
  We’re excited.

  The storm isn’t a threat.
  It’s wind in our sails.

   How do you approach uncertainty? Do you brace for it… or build for it?', null, 'posted', '2025-09-17T12:00:00Z', '2025-09-17T12:00:00Z');
insert into public.posts (id, campaign_id, theme, position, body, linkedin_url, status, scheduled_at, posted_at)
values ('6c178cd2-f61d-47e2-a77c-39c2d4fe1eba', '969b6828-7641-4509-9a99-50ec4877e157', 'The Human-First Rule of HealthTech', 8, 'I’ve seen it too many times in tech.
 A product gets built because it’s “cool” or “innovative”…
 …and then it just sits there.
 No one uses it.
 No one cares.

 Why?
 Because the team started with technology instead of people.

 At CipherCross, we have one golden rule:
 People first. Technology second.

 Before we write a single line of code, we make time for the messy part:

→ Talking to potential users.
→ Running focus groups.
→ Researching public opinion.
→ Listening more than we speak.

 Because technology without a clear human purpose is just code.
 And code, no matter how sophisticated, doesn’t change lives by itself.

 One of my favorite moments at CipherCross was when we built an AI-driven MVP in just
 12 hours.
 Sure, the “12 hours” part is exciting to talk about.
 But the real reason it worked wasn’t the speed - it was the fact that we started from
 a human need and worked backwards.

 We asked:

1) Who is this for?
2) What are they struggling with?
3) How can AI actually make their life easier - not just look impressive?

 This approach keeps us grounded.
 It makes sure that every product we launch is useful, usable, and lasting.

 Because if it’s not making someone’s life better, it doesn’t matter how advanced the tech is.

  What’s the last “shiny” tech you saw that nobody actually needed?

  P.S. Riding towards the next big milestone', null, 'posted', '2025-09-19T12:00:00Z', '2025-09-19T12:00:00Z');
