values ('6fe1cd20-0022-4dda-bd4f-a08d0e495b9f', '969b6828-7641-4509-9a99-50ec4877e157', 'Travel memory that still inspires me', 14, 'Format: Photo + short story  Type: Personal  Hook: “Some places stay with you long after the trip ends. For me, it was…”', null, 'posted', '2025-10-20T06:00:00Z', '2025-10-20T06:00:00Z');
insert into public.posts (id, campaign_id, theme, position, body, linkedin_url, status, scheduled_at, posted_at)
values ('87fb9b1c-f1de-4257-9d10-53a9971c1b95', '969b6828-7641-4509-9a99-50ec4877e157', 'Technology That Makes Us More Human', 15, 'If a wellness app makes you anxious about progress, it’s not wellness anymore.

I’ve always believed that technology should make people feel more human, not less.
It should give space to slow down, reflect, and reconnect with yourself, not rush you toward another achievement badge.

At CipherCross, we design every product with that in mind.
Every interaction, notification, and data visualization is crafted to support calmness and awareness, not pressure.

Because if your wellness app leaves you feeling tense or “behind,” something’s wrong with the design, not with you.

One story that stays with me is a user who shared that the short breathing rituals in one of our mindfulness apps helped them stop experiencing daily panic attacks.

It wasn’t a complex algorithm or a flashy interface that made the difference, but it was simplicity and emotional safety.

That’s the kind of impact we aim for.
To create tech that doesn’t compete for attention, but gives it back to the user.
To build products that remind people that wellness isn’t a race; it’s a relationship with yourself.

What makes a digital product truly supportive for you?', null, 'posted', '2025-10-22T06:00:00Z', '2025-10-22T06:00:00Z');
insert into public.posts (id, campaign_id, theme, position, body, linkedin_url, status, scheduled_at, posted_at)
values ('d83fd9b8-db5c-42ab-bc4e-0ae6fb89dde9', '969b6828-7641-4509-9a99-50ec4877e157', 'Weekend Reset: Practicing Calm Myself', 16, 'Before I build tools for calm, I make sure I practice it myself.

Working in the wellness tech space taught me one simple truth: you can’t create peace for others if you’re running on empty yourself.

My weekends are my reset ritual.
Sometimes it’s a quiet walk, or a short meditation, just being offline long enough to hear my own thoughts again.

It’s not about being perfectly balanced - it’s about returning to balance when life pulls you off track.
And I’ve learned that this pause directly influences how I lead, create, and make product decisions.

At CipherCross, we build digital experiences that help people slow down, reflect, and breathe.
But to design tools that truly feel supportive, I need to experience calm in my own routine first.

This weekend, I’ll be recharging,  not just for myself, but for the clarity it brings to everything I create next.

How do you recharge after a busy week?', null, 'posted', '2025-10-24T06:00:00Z', '2025-10-24T06:00:00Z');
insert into public.posts (id, campaign_id, theme, position, body, linkedin_url, status, scheduled_at, posted_at)
values ('b5d8b84b-b57b-497c-b2fe-614e08c3fa26', '969b6828-7641-4509-9a99-50ec4877e157', 'Building a Calmer Business, Not a Bigger One', 17, 'Growth feels better when it’s predictable, not chaotic.

For a long time, I chased the kind of growth that looked good on paper - more projects, more clients, more output. But somewhere between the late nights and the endless calls, I realized that “more” doesn’t always mean “better.”

In wellness tech and in business in general, there’s this unspoken pressure to scale fast. If you’re not expanding, hiring, shipping, fundraising… You feel like you’re falling behind.

But the truth?
Fast growth can be fragile. It stretches people, processes, and purpose.

At some point, I stopped asking “How can we grow faster?”
And started asking, “How can we grow calmer?”

That shift changed everything for me and for the company. This year, my goal isn’t to build a bigger company, but to build a calmer, more predictable one.

Here’s what that means in practice:
 → Predictable profit, not adrenaline-fueled peaks and valleys.
 → Repeatable sales pipelines, so growth feels steady, not accidental.
 → Meaningful case studies that tell the story of our work end-to-end - not just the highlights.
 → Room to think, not just react.

Calm doesn’t mean complacent; it means clarity, being able to zoom out, see the bigger picture, and make better decisions - for our team, our clients, and the users we ultimately serve.

And honestly, this mindset shift reflects what we build for others as well.
At CipherCross, we create wellness apps that help people slow down, reflect, and reconnect with themselves—responsibly, securely, and intentionally.
So it makes sense that we apply the same principle internally: scaling with calm and purpose.

If your business grows but your peace of mind disappears, something’s off. Founders rarely talk about calm scaling, but it’s the only kind that lasts.

Because when the work feels lighter and the process feels repeatable,  that’s when true growth starts to compound.

Here’s to building businesses that are not just bigger, but calmer, clearer, and kinder to the people behind them.

What would a “calmer version” of your work look like next year?', null, 'posted', '2025-10-27T06:00:00Z', '2025-10-27T06:00:00Z');
insert into public.posts (id, campaign_id, theme, position, body, linkedin_url, status, scheduled_at, posted_at)
values ('0ce72991-f292-48d5-a4a1-014b2978f4bd', '969b6828-7641-4509-9a99-50ec4877e157', 'What Inspires Me to Keep Creating', 18, 'Text Post Idea: Share what fuels your creativity — mission-driven founders, mentors, honest user feedback, travel, and the constraints of product design. Hook: “Inspiration rarely strikes from nowhere. It’s built from conversations, feedback, and quiet observation.” Format: Carousel or text post with bullet-style rhythm', null, 'posted', '2025-10-29T06:00:00Z', '2025-10-29T06:00:00Z');
insert into public.posts (id, campaign_id, theme, position, body, linkedin_url, status, scheduled_at, posted_at)
values ('47425e16-b952-40df-a2ec-71c6d6334c96', '969b6828-7641-4509-9a99-50ec4877e157', 'What True Data Privacy Looks Like in Wellness Apps', 19, 'Women aren’t designed to train the same way every day - energy, recovery, iron levels and cortisol response shift across the menstrual cycle. Yet most fitness and wellness apps still treat female bodies like a flat 28-day calendar.

At CipherCross, we design wellness apps that adapt to women, not the other way around.

Why it matters:
1) Generic training plans ignore hormonal changes
2) Leads to fatigue, burnout and “I’m failing” guilt
3) Cycle-based training improves strength, mood and adherence

Our approach:
✔ Workout + recovery tailored to menstrual phases
✔ Micro-adjustments, not one-size-fits-all plans
✔ Built with medical experts and privacy-first AI (data stays on-device)

Wellness shouldn’t ignore female biology.
 Learn more or partner with us → [ciphercross.com](http://ciphercross.com)', null, 'posted', '2025-10-31T06:00:00Z', '2025-10-31T06:00:00Z');
insert into public.posts (id, campaign_id, theme, position, body, linkedin_url, status, scheduled_at, posted_at)
values ('0467039f-e96d-4567-b73d-2ea825f24d0c', '969b6828-7641-4509-9a99-50ec4877e157', 'Why I believe in wellness-driven tech', 20, 'Not every challenge deserves your energy - especially if it’s driven by ego, not purpose.

Over the years, I’ve learned to say “no” more often - not because I’m afraid of challenges, but because I’ve become more intentional about which ones I take on.

In wellness tech, it’s easy to get caught up in vanity metrics - downloads, engagement spikes, shiny dashboards.
But when the goal shifts from helping people feel better to looking bigger, something important gets lost.

I’ve made a simple rule for myself:
If a project helps move the wellness space forward - or helps people connect more meaningfully with themselves - I’m all in.
But if it’s just about growth for the sake of growth, I’ll pass.

One story that still keeps me grounded is from a user of one of our mindfulness apps.
They shared that a simple breathing ritual we designed helped them stop daily panic attacks.
No gamification, no complex UX — just a calm space that made them feel safe.
That’s the kind of impact that matters.

In a world full of noise, choosing integrity over ego is the real challenge worth taking.

  How do you decide which challenges are truly worth your time?', null, 'posted', '2025-11-03T06:00:00Z', '2025-11-03T06:00:00Z');
insert into public.posts (id, campaign_id, theme, position, body, linkedin_url, status, scheduled_at, posted_at)
values ('25c308a3-d701-4d7f-921b-68998c1062b4', '969b6828-7641-4509-9a99-50ec4877e157', 'How I Decide Which Challenges Are Worth My Time', 21, 'Type: LI Personal Knowledge Hook: “I say ‘no’ to more projects than I say ‘yes.’ Here’s my rule.” Text Post Idea: Explain the decision framework (scorecard 0–3 for 5 criteria + automatic disqualifiers). Break into simple bullets: strategic fit, portfolio value, learning delta, ROI, energy check. Add “non-negotiables: no privacy-risk projects, no weak ownership, no vague decisions.” Format: Text + Slide graphic “My 5-point Challenge Scorecard”', 'https://www.linkedin.com/posts/mykyta-shevchenko-ciphercross_in-wellness-tech-collecting-less-data-isn-activity-7440333761055125504-xKyX?utm_source=share&utm_medium=member_desktop&rcm=ACoAAD1GaXQBLC3IwzuWDrLQoIsrw5Y1tC2y1Xs', 'posted', '2026-03-19T07:15:00Z', '2026-03-19T07:15:00Z');
insert into public.post_stats (post_id, views, likes, comments, recorded_at)
values ('25c308a3-d701-4d7f-921b-68998c1062b4', 209, 7, 0, '2026-06-02T12:00:00Z');
insert into public.posts (id, campaign_id, theme, position, body, linkedin_url, status, scheduled_at, posted_at)
values ('4cd8651b-3e2a-4884-a21b-edceb3918d09', '969b6828-7641-4509-9a99-50ec4877e157', 'Small Wins, Big Impact – My Ritual of Celebrating Progress', 22, 'When you’re building a wellness-focused company, most progress happens quietly.

No headlines.
No big launch days.
Just small, consistent wins.

Over the past year, I learned that if I wait for the “big moment” to feel proud, I’ll burn out long before I ever get there.
So I built a simple ritual to notice progress before it disappears into the next task.

→ How I celebrate small wins:

1. Espresso walk by the river in Lisbon
10 quiet minutes. No Slack. No strategy thinking. Just breathing, coffee and sunlight.

2. “Wins log” in Notion
Every Friday I write down 3 things that moved us forward - a solved bug, a grateful client, or even a clearer idea. It keeps momentum visible.

3. Public shoutouts in Slack
If someone on the team makes a breakthrough, I don’t wait for performance reviews. I drop a thank-you note in Slack right away. Energy is contagious.

Why does this matter?

Big goals like predictable profitability, flagship case studies, or scaling wellness apps don’t happen overnight. They are built on hundreds of small, unglamorous steps. Celebrating them it’s fuel, because sustainable growth isn’t about speed, but it’s about consistency + gratitude.

How do you celebrate your small wins?
I’d love to steal your ritual.', 'https://www.linkedin.com/posts/mykyta-shevchenko-ciphercross_when-youre-building-a-wellness-focused-company-activity-7441759408054927360-trau?utm_source=share&utm_medium=member_desktop&rcm=ACoAAD1GaXQBLC3IwzuWDrLQoIsrw5Y1tC2y1Xs', 'posted', '2026-03-23T06:15:00Z', '2026-03-23T06:15:00Z');
